 \begin{abstract}
-Light carries data and signatures of the path and objects it encounters,
-allowing the same optical infrastructure to support communication and sensing.
-Optical integrated sensing and communication (O-ISAC) builds on this dual
-capability across fiber, free space optical, visible light, and light fidelity
-(LiFi) platforms. Photonic hardware extends the concept to millimeter wave and
-terahertz systems, while hybrid designs connect physical domains. This breadth
-motivates comparison of the design insights that extend
-across platforms and those tied to a particular physical setting. A systematic
-literature search covering 1 January 2020 through 22 June 2026 identified 227
-eligible reports representing 206 unique studies. We organized this evidence by
-physical platform and coupling mechanism using structured narrative synthesis
-and a technical assessment developed for this review. The comparison framework
-connects each result to its task, measurement plane, operating conditions, and
-validation setting. Across the included corpus, bandwidth, optical power,
-hardware constraints, and signal design jointly shape communication reliability
-and sensing performance. These relationships depend on reported conditions and
-extend beyond a single rate and resolution frontier. Laboratory studies span
-diverse architectures. Broader field validation, shared benchmarks, and
-reusable data and code emerge as priorities for stronger comparison and
-reconstruction. The resulting taxonomy and framework support the design and
-evaluation of O-ISAC systems for sixth generation (6G) networks.
+Optical integrated sensing and communication (O-ISAC) reuses optical
+infrastructure, signals, or hardware to transmit data and observe a physical
+environment. Its implementations span guided fiber, optical wireless, and
+photonics-enabled millimeter-wave and terahertz links, whose different sensing
+paths complicate the interpretation of reported performance. This systematic
+review synthesizes 227 eligible reports representing 206 studies published
+from January 2020 through June 2026. We connect each architecture's shared
+resource to the design variable, physical mechanism, and communication and
+sensing outcomes it affects, retaining source-specific measurement and
+validation conditions. The synthesis identifies distinct routes to joint
+performance: reference allocation can reduce payload opportunity; increased
+probe power can improve sensing while impairing data recovery; and interference
+suppression or sensing-guided steering can support communication without the
+same resource exchange. These mechanisms explain why separately reported peak
+rates and sensing errors cannot define a common performance frontier. Laboratory
+and prototype evidence is broad, but only 12 studies reach field or deployment
+settings, with six reporting outcomes in both functional domains at that level.
+That coverage alone does not establish concurrent joint operation. We derive
+application-specific tests of traffic continuity, motion, optical drift, and
+inference cost, using paired measurements and separate-function baselines to
+determine when a demonstrated subsystem benefit remains useful in a network.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \begin{IEEEkeywords}
-6G, integrated sensing and communication, metric comparability, optical
-communication, optical sensing.
+6G, integrated sensing and communication, optical communication, optical
+sensing, resource allocation, systematic review.
 \end{IEEEkeywords}
